ive  and an aggressive treatment on the  patient and um but also the patient  should not be too sick otherwise they  cannot survive the open-heart surgery  so there's this very tricky task of  identifying this right window of  sickness and white right time to implant  and this is a very difficult decision  um so the idea is that machine learning  can help if we have  um almost about a decade of data about  the outcome of previous implant patients  um the idea is that if we can learn what  kind of  features what kind of factors that can  predict better treatment outcome  then we can systematically identify  people who are most likely to benefit  from the treatment these systems um  here's a screenshot from  such a system from 2008 and i have to  say systems today look roughly the same  so  you put in a list of patient variables  in electronic medical records and then  you click enter and you see  predictions of  the likely outcome  of this surgery  now  over the years we have research has  repeated show uh showed that doctors  don't use this kind of system among the  many buttons and many functions in  electronic medical records this is the  least used one  not an easy  achievement to to reach um and a common  complaint is that their experiences are  better than ai  and i think i think they have a really  good point um  one of the things when we work with  those doctors  the doctors are often asked is  are these outcomes are these previous  outcomes based on my best effort you  know i performed less well if i had a  fight with my wife last night  um and this kind of nuances might be  very strange for us for people who build  ai systems but for doctors they really  know it they know all the limitations of  the data set  on top on top of that and  they know there are limitations of the  ai so they really resisted this kind of  systems  so our goal is just trying to find that  kind of middle ground like how can we  make these systems useful knowing that  there have been problems among the  doctors like human decisions um but also  to to understand  um how these um how they can complement  each other so we went to three hospitals  across the u.s and we spend about 14  full days in their advanced heart  failure programs observing how these  clinicians take care of patients and  make decisions  um  i think well we found many barriers that  would prevent this ai from being useful  um  but one of the key  takeaway for us was  there is just really a lot of human  messiness in that decision  i'll give you one example so one of the  clinical guideline  guidelines is that  the patient should be given more  consideration  if they have a spouse if they have  someone who can  um who can take care of them in this  recovery process of the open heart  surgery um so the doctors will be  struggling with for example  i know this patient is about to have a  divorce but on paper this person is  still married so it actually meets the  standard to to to have this to to be  eligible for this treatment do i tell it  tell them or not these are the very  human decisions that the doctors try to  make and the way they make this decision  is not that let me see whether this fits  the guideline or not or what how ethical  it is to you know to to  manipulate a little bit about that  person's marital relationship instead  they're thinking how can i save this  patient this is a last stop treatment if  i say no  these patients go through palliative  care so for them the decision is not  really to consider eligibility they're  considering do i have a better  alternative if no  maybe i should just try it regardless  how high the risk this treatment is so  in dealing with all these very messy  conditions  what we found is doctors almost never  make decisions in front of a computer  they always make decisions especially  the difficult ones through conversations  i put this image because i cannot take  any photo in my healthcare empirical  work  but this is really what the doctors  meeting reminded me of they're like four  chandeliers over your head there is no  big projectors like this because in our  environment discussions are centered  around computers but for the doctors the  decisions are all centered around  human beings and the human beings are  the clinicians in this case are very are  working in a very hierarchical  organization people who  are attending physicians people who have  the power to sign up  clinical decisions set on one end of the  table and then you sit according to  ranks so people who the social workers  the nurse practitioners  um the interns who actually  take care of the patient in and out  almost 24 hours a day those people sit  on the very far end of the table and  they don't get  much to say in the meaning even though  they really have very sort of felt  understanding of what this patient  condition has been  so with all these insights in mind  we  started to consider a different kind of  ai um  so the idea is that instead of thinking  about our ai as something that can  inform the decision of implant or no  implant itself we want to think about ai  as something  that provides  like one component of that decision so  um here is the model we end up  retraining um  instead of predicting whether you should  or should not implant your or or  survival um  the the the  longevity um at post surgery we predict  the likelihood of every adversarial  event um  what does the chance of um renal failure  look like does that have all kinds of  implications  for not only  the implant  eligibility but also the quality of life  of that patient  and we embedded this machine learning  prediction it's in a corner here um  into this meeting slide  the idea is that on this slide every  component represents one facet of that  patient's condition here's some medical  history here's that person's financial  evaluation here's a person's  psychosocial evaluation maybe there is a  clinical note about that person's  marital status as well um the idea is  that ai is really not trying to be  at the front and center instead it's  just one another piece of information  that you should consider in the clinical  care process um  one doctor puts it very elegantly like  ai would just be  a blood test or an x-ray in the future  no doctor makes a decision solely based  on blood tests or solely based on  an x-ray result  it's always the big picture but that's  but just like x-ray and just like blood  test ai is a really important component  in this  we tested this system across three  hospitals in the u.s at different three  hospitals  and and we really found um  really encouraging results doctors  really embrace these predictions and  they actually use the ai  discuss spend a lot of time deliberating  what the ai means how much can you trust  a prediction versus a fact where i mean  everything in clinical  decision making is about you know  clinical trials and causal effects like  how do we deal with this prediction  so that elaboration was really helpful  and also it provides a lot of vigilance  for the people who sit at the far end of  that long table they can say hey doctor  i don't agree with you here is an ai  that also says well they don't need to  say i don't agree with it they can say  hey doctor why don't why do you think  the ai you know says this and so it  triggers a social conversation and in  that sense it's really  um it really provided more perspectives  in that discussion  we have evidence that this kind of  message um and this kind of design this  idea that ai being one faces of clinical  decision really generalizes to all kinds  of  um  complex clinical decisions that involve  multiple organs or usually involves  multiple groups of clinicians  and here is just another quick case  study where this is a collaboration with  google brain and google health where we  apply the exact same message to  cancer diagnosis on pathology slides by  the way this is how a clinical best  practice looks like this is what the  doctors are making your cancer diagnosis  based on really you know beautiful art  um  and we found that doctors make these  decisions really based on gut reaction  they can  they can diagnose they can see cancer on  that slide which is only twice as large  as our actual cells and they can see  cancer from there so that really sort of  instinctive  um image recognition skill when  complemented by ai we can improve the  cancer diagnosis  accuracy by about 30 percent so that's a  really encouraging  result  and i also want to mention other  scholars work that  over time have discovered similar  patterns in other  critical domains like civic uh  civil litigation and  artists working with language models and  so on so forth  what does this all mean  um  i want to share three  ideas i think are really mainly for  provocations i'm curious what you guys  all think  um  point number one  i think when we discuss the  imperfections of ai we should always  talk about them  side by side with human limitations  just like human beings data and the ai  built upon it always captures humanities  or always capture human needs  in a quite partial and quite reductive  way  but that's the same as how doctors make  decisions they cannot possibly think  about  every possible phases of the situation  in order to make decisions you have to  be reflective in some way  so  i think  it's relatively fine in a lot of cases  it's fine for ai to be partial and  reductive  but the key is that we recognize these  limitations we recognize how these ais  are partial and are reductive and  deliberate  these limitations alongside with the  human perspectives as long as there is  this deliberation most often we can get  good results as shown in all the case  studies i mentioned earlier  but then here comes a real new question  i think the real challenge of making ai  like accounting for ai's unintended  consequences is really people's limited  attention um i think it is not a  coincidence that some of the most  damaging ai outcomes come from seemingly  trivial things like social media reading  social media feeds youtube videos where  you see cute cat videos  rather than medicine  law these seemingly really you know  remarkable places that is because  doctors and lawyers and so forth spend a  lot of time deliberating um  they recognize the ai's limitations and  when they are open enough to use it they  know how to put it in perspective  with their own pers uh where's their  personal understandings  but in a lot of other cases people don't  have the attention people don't  deliberate or people cannot even imagine  how the ai can be wrong and i think  those are the places that are really  dangerous  one last point um  i think we're living in a society where  deliberating on each and every one each  and every ai is becoming increasingly  impossible um  we um our group currently does a lot of  work in text generation and writing  among everyday people not artists so  people who don't have the time to  deliberate on each whether each ai  suggestion mean  and for people who work on platform tags  where  like think about google search that has  which has more than a thousand ai  systems running in the background you  cannot deliberate on each limitation of  each ai and that  makes it really hard for us to think  about human ai complementarity think  about how you can make this process  deliberative and thoughtful and how can  we prevent biases in these contexts are  the real challenge for  our next step  thank you

